================================================================================
                  EX02 — No, you need form 28B, not 28C...
================================================================================

WHAT THIS EXERCISE COVERS:
  - Abstract classes (AForm replaces Form from ex01)
  - Pure virtual functions (virtual ... = 0)
  - Concrete derived classes inheriting from an abstract base
  - Polymorphism: calling derived execute() through base pointer/reference
  - File I/O (ShrubberyCreationForm writes to a file)
  - Bureaucrat::executeForm() — a method that calls form.execute()
  - Three concrete form classes with hard-coded grade requirements:
      ShrubberyCreationForm:   sign 145, exec 137 → writes ASCII trees to file
      RobotomyRequestForm:    sign 72,  exec 45  → 50% chance robotomy
      PresidentialPardonForm:  sign 25,  exec 5   → pardoned by Zaphod

GRADE SYSTEM REMINDER:
  - Grade 1   = HIGHEST (best)
  - Grade 150 = LOWEST  (worst)
  - A bureaucrat can SIGN   if grade <= gradeToSign
  - A bureaucrat can EXECUTE if grade <= gradeToExecute AND form is signed

================================================================================
                       REMAINING BUGS IN YOUR CODE
================================================================================

[BUG 1] AForm.hpp — Wrong include guard: FORM_HPP instead of AFORM_HPP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code:
      #ifndef FORM_HPP
      #define FORM_HPP

  Should be:
      #ifndef AFORM_HPP
      #define AFORM_HPP

  You renamed the class to AForm but forgot to update the guard.
  This could cause subtle issues if another file uses FORM_HPP.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 2] AForm.hpp — Getters and beSigned() should NOT be pure virtual
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code makes ALL methods pure virtual (= 0):
      virtual int getGradeToExecute() const = 0;
      virtual int getGradeToSign() const = 0;
      virtual std::string getName() const = 0;
      virtual bool isitSigned() const = 0;
      virtual void beSigned(const Bureaucrat& b) = 0;

  PROBLEM:
    - These getters access PRIVATE members of AForm. Derived classes
      can't access them, so they can't re-implement these methods.
    - beSigned() logic is the same for all forms — no need to override.
    - Only execute() should be pure virtual (it's the one that differs).

  Should be:
      int getGradeToExecute() const;
      int getGradeToSign() const;
      std::string getName() const;
      bool isitSigned() const;
      void beSigned(const Bureaucrat& b);
      virtual void execute(Bureaucrat const & executor) const = 0;

  The "= 0" on execute() is what makes AForm abstract, not the getters.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 3] AForm.hpp — Missing execute() pure virtual method
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The whole point of making AForm abstract is the execute() method.
  Each concrete form overrides execute() with different behavior.

  Add to the public section:
      virtual void execute(Bureaucrat const & executor) const = 0;

  Also add a protected helper to check execution requirements:
      void checkExecutionRequirements(Bureaucrat const & executor) const;

  This helper checks: (1) form is signed, (2) executor grade is high enough.
  All three concrete classes call it at the start of their execute().

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 4] AForm.hpp — Destructor is not virtual
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code:
      ~AForm();

  Should be:
      virtual ~AForm();

  RULE: If a class has virtual methods, its destructor MUST be virtual.
  Without this, deleting a derived object through an AForm* pointer
  won't call the derived destructor → memory leak / undefined behavior.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 5] Bureaucrat — Missing executeForm() method
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The subject requires Bureaucrat to have:
      void executeForm(AForm const & form);

  This method should:
    1. Try to call form.execute(*this)
    2. On success: print "<name> executed <form name>"
    3. On exception: print "<name> couldn't execute <form name> because <reason>"

  Add declaration to Bureaucrat.hpp and implementation in Bureaucrat.cpp.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 6] Bureaucrat::signaAForm — Missing exception handling
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your code:
      void Bureaucrat::signaAForm(AForm& f){
          f.beSigned(*this);
          if (f.isitSigned())
              std::cout << name << " signed " << f.getName() << std::endl;
      }

  PROBLEM: If beSigned() throws, there's no catch block — the exception
  propagates up unhandled. The subject says signForm should print a failure
  message, not crash.

  Should be:
      void Bureaucrat::signForm(AForm& f){
          try {
              f.beSigned(*this);
              std::cout << name << " signed " << f.getName() << std::endl;
          } catch (std::exception& e) {
              std::cout << name << " couldn't sign " << f.getName()
                        << " because " << e.what() << std::endl;
          }
      }

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[BUG 7] Bureaucrat copy constructor — Useless self-check (same as ex00/ex01)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Still has:
      if (&other != this){ this->grade = other.getGrade(); }

  Clean version:
      Bureaucrat::Bureaucrat(const Bureaucrat& other)
          : name(other.name), grade(other.grade) {}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

