================================================================================
                            TRAPS / PITFALLS
================================================================================

  TRAP 1: Pure virtual vs regular virtual
    "= 0" means the method MUST be overridden by derived classes.
    Only put "= 0" on methods that genuinely differ per form (execute).
    Getters that access base class data should NOT be pure virtual.

  TRAP 2: Virtual destructor is MANDATORY
    Any class with virtual methods needs a virtual destructor.
    Without it: AForm* f = new ShrubberyCreationForm("home");
    delete f; → only calls ~AForm(), not ~ShrubberyCreationForm() → LEAK.

  TRAP 3: execute() must check TWO things before running
    1. Is the form signed? If not → throw
    2. Is the executor's grade high enough? If not → throw
    Put this in a shared helper so all 3 forms use the same check.

  TRAP 4: ShrubberyCreationForm file writing
    The file name is "<target>_shrubbery" (no extension).
    Use std::ofstream. Check if the file opened before writing.

  TRAP 5: Concrete forms pass their grades to AForm constructor
    Each concrete class calls AForm(name, gradeToSign, gradeToExecute)
    in its initializer list. The grades are HARD-CODED per class:
      ShrubberyCreationForm("Shrubbery Creation", 145, 137)
      RobotomyRequestForm("Robotomy Request", 72, 45)
      PresidentialPardonForm("Presidential Pardon", 25, 5)

  TRAP 6: const in execute() signature
    execute(Bureaucrat const & executor) const — the method is CONST.
    This means execute() cannot modify the form's state.
    This is fine because execute() only reads isSigned and grades.